<%#
  One collapsible cross-cutting group of the project Files panel — Loose, Backups and Linked
  from elsewhere (a real subfolder renders through _file_tree_section.html.erb instead, since
  stage 3, S3-1 made sections the folder's own subfolders rather than a role derived from the
  filename).

  Locals: key (a stable slug for the section, unused below but kept for a caller that wants one),
  label, rows (Maker::ProjectFolderListing::Row), open (Boolean), view (one of
  Maker::Project::VIEWS — the ENCLOSING PANEL's, passed down; see below).

  A ROW IS A FOLDER FILE OR A LINKED ATTACHMENT, AND row.linked? IS THE ONLY BRANCH IN THIS
  PARTIAL (inside _file_tile.html.erb, which owns it). A folder row's node comes straight from the
  listing's own walk and carries no attachment at all (row.attachment is nil) — there is nothing
  to detach and nowhere else in the library the file already lives, so its tile is the thumbnail
  alone. A linked row's node is attached to this project but living somewhere else in the library,
  so its tile also carries a corner: Detach (severs the reference; the file itself is kept) and
  Move into project (Maker::AttachToProject#relocate under the file's own name — the same door the
  collision prompt's "Move it in as…" button posts to, in _move_conflict.html.erb).

  TILES OR LIST, AS THE ENCLOSING PANEL WAS TOLD (`view`, a REQUIRED local since 2026-09-08) —
  _file_rows.html.erb draws the same rows as a table when the panel is in list mode, so
  Loose/Backups/Linked switch exactly the way a folder panel's own files do. It was `@view`, one
  page-wide cookie, until the view became per panel (spec decision 6): an ivar cannot say WHICH
  panel is asking, and a group renders inside exactly one.

  `open` is a real HTML attribute either way, so a closed section costs nothing at render time and
  the browser keeps the toggle state for the life of the page. It is NOT persisted across loads:
  that would need a cookie per section per project, and the panel's default (everything open but
  Backups) is the answer for almost every visit.

  NATIVE <details>, NOT A STIMULUS CONTROLLER: collapsing is what the element is for, it works
  before any JavaScript loads, and `models/_file_tree.html.erb` already uses it for exactly this.
%>
<section class="attachment-group">
  <details <%= "open" if open %>>
    <summary class="attachment-group-head">
      <%= label %> <span class="muted">(<%= rows.size %>)</span>
    </summary>
    <% if view == "list" %>
      <%= render "file_rows", rows: rows %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="attachments-grid">
        <%= render partial: "file_tile", collection: rows, as: :row %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </details>
</section>
